<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="indole-3-acetate activation I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-1822" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group, and, enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The phytohormone family of auxins regulates many plant biological processes including cell division, elongation, differentiation, root initiation, tropic responses, flowering, fruit ripening, and senescence |CITS:[15749753]|. |FRAME: INDOLE_ACETATE_AUXIN &quot;Indole-3-acetic acid&quot;| (IAA) is the most abundant natural plant auxin. In addition to &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; synthesis, IAA can be released from its conjugates. Amide-linked conjugates of IAA have been identified in most plant species. They function in storage, inactivation or inhibition of the growth regulator auxin. In Arabidopsis, the naturally occurring IAA amide conjugates |FRAME: INDOLE-3-ACETYL-LEU| and |FRAME: INDOLE-3-ACETYL-ALA| have been reported to be hydrolyzed to release free IAA |CITS: [Bartel01][Kowalczyk02]|. The enzymes responsible for the hydrolysis reside in the endoplasmic reticulum |CITS: [27063913]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="nilo-poyanco" />
